Include the header that knows how to format a byte.

hrtf_loader.cpp prints the four bytes of an unrecognised HRTF header as
"{::#04X}" over std::as_bytes(...), and includes fmt/core.h and
fmt/ranges.h. That was enough while the std::byte formatter lived in
fmt/format.h. fmt 12.2.0 moved it to fmt/std.h -- "Moved the std::byte
formatter from fmt/format.h to fmt/std.h" -- and without it the format
string check fails at compile time, on a message about a specifier:

    error: 'unknown format specifier'
      in constexpr expansion of fstring<span<const byte, 8>>("{::#04X}")

So the header that has the formatter is included. fmt/core.h becomes
fmt/format.h in the same file, which is what upstream did on master and
what fmt 12.2 requires of anyone calling fmt::format: core.h now includes
base.h alone.

Both headers exist in every fmt this can be built against, so the file
compiles the same way against the copy in the tree.

--- a/core/hrtf_loader.cpp
+++ b/core/hrtf_loader.cpp
@@ -13,8 +13,9 @@
 
 #include "alformat.hpp"
 #include "alnumeric.h"
-#include "fmt/core.h"
+#include "fmt/format.h"
 #include "fmt/ranges.h"
+#include "fmt/std.h"
 #include "gsl/gsl"
 #include "hrtf.h"
 
